<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isaac Singer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1811">1811</a>–<a href="/years/1875">1875</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Isaac Merritt Singer, Isaac Merritt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Isaac Merritt Singer was an American inventor, actor and businessman who improved the design of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machine</a> and founded what became the Singer Sewing Machine Company, one of the first American multi-national businesses. After patenting a rock drill in 1839 and touring as an actor, he redesigned a sewing machine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> so that the shuttle moved in a straight line, with a straight needle, and patented it in 1851. In 1856 his firm joined the Sewing Machine Combination, the first patent pool, and sewing machines began to be mass-produced and marketed for home use. He settled in Paignton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, in 1871 and died in 1875, leaving a $13 million fortune.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isaac-singer.jpg" alt="Isaac Singer" width="330" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Harrison May · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edward_Harrison_May_-_Isaac_Merrit_Singer_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Isaac Merritt Singer was born on 27 October 1811 in the hamlet of Johnsonville, in the town of Pittstown, the youngest of eight children of the mill builder Adam Singer, a German from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, and his American wife Ruth Benson. His parents divorced in 1821 and his mother left him. At twelve he ran away from home to join a travelling stage act, the Rochester Players, after finding bits of work as a joiner and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lathe">lathe</a> operator. In 1830, at nineteen, he married fifteen-year-old Catherine Maria Haley. He fathered 26 children by five different women.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1839 Singer obtained his first patent, for a rock-drilling machine, which he sold for $2,000. He then toured for about five years as an actor with the Merritt Players. In 1850 he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> to display a wood-carving machine at Orson C. Phelps's shop, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sewing-machine">sewing machines</a> were being repaired. He concluded they would be more reliable if the shuttle moved in a straight line and the needle was straight, and obtained US Patent number 8294 on 12 August 1851. Many others, among them Walter Hunt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elias-howe">Elias Howe</a>, had patented sewing machines earlier, and his success rested on the practicality of his machine, its adaptability to home use and sale by instalments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>I. M. Singer &amp; Co made 2,564 machines in 1856 and 13,000 in 1860. In 1863 the firm was dissolved by mutual consent, and the business continued as The Singer Manufacturing Company with Singer holding 40% of the shares. In 1871 he settled in Paignton, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>, and commissioned the 110-roomed Oldway Mansion. Singer died on 23 July 1875 and divided his $13 million fortune unequally among 20 of his living children, one of whom, William, received $500. The machine sewed 900 stitches a minute against some 40 for an accomplished seamstress. A factory at Clydebank near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>, built between 1882 and 1885, opened with 3,500 workers.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Isaac Singer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Isaac Singer</b> received a US patent for a sewing machine with an up-and-down needle and foot treadle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
